Write BAR addresses through to the VFIO device.

Cloud Hypervisor sizes each BAR by writing all ones into the device's
config space and then keeps the guest's BAR programming in a local shadow
only. vfio-pci does not care: it virtualises BAR writes. NVIDIA's vGPU
vendor driver emulates the virtual function's config space itself and
takes the BAR register as the guest's BAR base. It was left at
0xfffffff0, so every BAR0 register read reached the manager at
0xfffffff0 + offset ("No space found for read", "Register read failed
... Timeout") and the guest driver reported the GPU as unsupported.

Write the allocated address after sizing and forward every later guest
BAR write, which is what QEMU does with all config writes.

--- a/pci/src/vfio.rs
